{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788306611,"nonce":"sTMC3JEZtokxMXO85bpbULwApNc=","title":"accept/fc287ba 0.7c cannot tell a refusal that ran the clock from a bench that arrived running — 390 ticks blamed on TimeDriver","message":"Found on a bench on 2026-09-01 (session 21), while verifying that `1113019` had not regressed this suite. **It had not. 0.7c fired anyway, and the interesting part is that it cannot say which of two things it caught.**\n\n## What happened\n\n    0.7a  PASS  every refusal left the game PAUSED (data.time.paused)\n    0.7b  PASS  …at speed Paused, not merely force-paused (data.time.speed)\n    0.7c  FAIL  …and the tick did not advance across nine refusals\n              expected: tick unchanged at 849\n              actual:   1239\n\n390 ticks. The obvious reading — \"a refusal took the clock off Paused and left it running\" — is the one the check is written to detect, and it is the reading the failure hands you.\n\n## That reading is refuted, two ways\n\n**STRUCTURALLY.** `AgentGameComponent.DrainCommands` is a `while (Runtime.Pending.TryDequeue(...))` loop, and `TimeDriver.Start` runs to completion inside one iteration of it, inside one `GameComponentUpdate`. No frame boundary — and therefore no `TickManagerUpdate` — falls between `SetSpeed(tm, want, \"advance start\")` and the `RestorePause()` that every arm-time refusal unwinds through. There is no `yield`, no `await`, no `LongEventHandler` anywhere in that call graph. The refusal cannot leak a tick, and the correct expectation is exactly zero rather than \"a few\".\n\n**EMPIRICALLY.** The same nine refusals are banked from the s19 run: `accept/runs/s19-20260901/envelopes/accfc287ba-003-digest.json` reads `tick 1118, paused true, speed \"Paused\"` and `-016-digest.json` — the 0.7 read, after eight `bad-args` advance envelopes — reads `tick 1118, paused true, speed \"Paused\"`. Zero drift, and `suite.txt` line 44 records `0.7c PASS`.\n\n## What the check actually cannot distinguish\n\n0.7c takes its baseline from `S[\"digest\"]`, captured at check **0.3**, and phase 0 never asserts the game is paused at that moment. The suite's own header says *\"leave it paused\"* — and nothing checks.\n\nSo a bench that arrived RUNNING produces this failure exactly:\n\n* the clock ticks from 0.3 through 0.4 (status), 0.5a–0.5d (four `construction` calls) and 0.6a/0.6b (which throw in the `until` parse, BEFORE `SetSpeed`, so they never call `RestorePause`);\n* **0.6c is the first refusal that reaches `Arm()`**, and its unwind calls `RestorePause()`, which pauses the game;\n* everything after that is paused, so 0.7a and 0.7b pass;\n* only the delta survives.\n\nEight protocol round trips at the documented 0.25–1 s floor each (`rwa/README.md`: 500 ms poller, inbox files younger than 250 ms ignored) is 4–8 s, and at ~60 tps that is 240–480 ticks. The observed 390 sits in the middle of that window.\n\n**0.7a and 0.7b passing while 0.7c fails is the signature.** If a refusal were leaving the clock running, the LAST refusal would leave it running too and 0.7a would fail.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than shrugging at\n\nThis is the class of defect this project keeps paying for: a check whose conclusion depends on a precondition it does not assert. It reports a driver regression when the real event is a bench that was handed over in the wrong state. Two sessions in a row could chase `TimeDriver` for it. One already did — the attribution cost a full read of `Start`, `DrainCommands`, `RestorePause`, the decompiled `Verse/TickManager.TogglePaused`/`Pause`, and the s19 envelope bank, to establish that nothing was wrong.\n\nIt is also the more dangerous direction of the two errors. A confounded check does not just cry wolf: on a bench that IS paused it would still catch a genuine leak, so nobody deletes it — they just learn to disbelieve it.\n\n## The fix\n\nAssert the precondition where the baseline is taken, and measure the refusals in isolation. `digest.time` already publishes `paused` and `speed`, so it is one check and no new surface:\n\n* at **0.3**, assert `data.time.paused is True` and `data.time.speed == \"Paused\"` before capturing `S[\"digest\"]` — as a `precondition()` (exit 2, \"a FIXTURE gap, not a failure of the spec\") rather than a `check()`, because a bench handed over running is exactly that;\n* take the 0.7c baseline immediately BEFORE 0.6a rather than at 0.3, so the four `construction` calls and the `status` call are outside the measured window;\n* keep 0.7c's assertion unchanged — zero is the right expectation and the reasoning above is why.\n\n`accept/1113019-until-bound.py` (session 21, second round) does this for the `unreachable-halt` path already: `pause_and_read()` pauses, re-reads, and makes the paused-ness its own numbered check, so a FAIL on the following `clock_unmoved()` has one meaning. That helper and its `--selftest` discriminators (9.8/9.9) are a working model; this issue is the same treatment for fc287ba.\n\n**Deliberately not patched by the worker who found it**, for two reasons: 0.7c is a real check that caught a real bench-state problem and editing it mid-sweep would invalidate the run it is part of, and a change to a shared suite's assertions with no bench to re-run it on is a change nobody can verify.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Phase 0 asserts, BEFORE it captures the digest that 0.7c compares against, that the game is paused AND at speed `Paused`. A bench handed over running exits 2 with \"a FIXTURE gap, not a failure of the spec\", naming the tick and the speed it found.\n- 0.7c's baseline is taken immediately before the first refusal, so no non-advance command sits inside the measured window.\n- 0.7c still expects a delta of exactly ZERO, and its failure message reports the delta with its sign.\n- Proven to DISCRIMINATE, not just to pass: an offline check (the suite has no `--selftest` yet, so this may be its first) shows the new precondition REJECTING a synthetic running-clock digest and a synthetic force-paused-but-speed-Ultrafast digest, and ACCEPTING a paused one. A guard that cannot be shown to fail is not a guard.\n- Re-run on a bench that is deliberately left RUNNING: the suite exits 2 naming the fixture gap, rather than reporting a driver regression.\n- Re-run on a paused bench: 0.7a/b/c all PASS, matching the s19 bank.","files":null}]}